---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/advanced/semiconductors-and-doping">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Metals conduct, insulators do not — and between those two poles lies the class of
    materials the modern world is built from. A <strong>semiconductor</strong> conducts a
    little at room temperature, much more when warmed, lit or doped; its conductivity is not
    a fixed property but a <em>design variable</em>, tunable over ten orders of magnitude by
    parts-per-million chemistry. This lesson covers the two syllabus lectures —
    <strong>semiconductors</strong> (why crystals have band gaps, and what intrinsic
    conductivity really is) and <strong>doping</strong> (how a foreign atom per few million
    becomes an engineered conductor) — and then builds the junction, because that is where
    control becomes computation.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="band gaps, doping, the junction, the fab chain" widget="equationStep" config={semiCfg} />

  <h2>Band gaps: why crystals choose conductors</h2>
  <p>
    In an isolated atom, electrons occupy discrete energy levels. Bring 10²³ atoms into a
    crystal and each level splays into a <strong>band</strong> — a quasi-continuous range of
    energies (think of 10²³ closely spaced tuning forks detuning each other). Bands may
    overlap, or be separated by <strong>band gaps</strong> — energy ranges no electron may
    hold. Conductivity is bookkeeping on bands: the highest filled band
    (<strong>valence</strong>) and the next empty one (<strong>conduction</strong>) decide
    everything. A <strong>metal</strong> has a half-filled band, so electrons near the top
    can drift freely. An <strong>insulator</strong> (diamond, 5.5 eV gap) has a full valence
    band and a huge gap — no electron can be promoted, nothing conducts. A
    <strong>semiconductor</strong> is the middle case: silicon's gap is 1.12 eV (germanium
    0.66, GaAs 1.42, 4H-SiC 3.26, GaN 3.4), small enough that thermal energy occasionally
    promotes an electron — leaving a vacancy in the valence band, the
    <strong>hole</strong>, which conducts exactly like a positive charge (the population
    moving the opposite way). At 300 K thermal energy is only kT ≈ 0.026 eV, forty times
    smaller than silicon's gap, so promotion is rare: the intrinsic carrier concentration
    follows nᵢ = √(N_c·N_v)·exp(−E_g/2kT), giving nᵢ ≈ 10¹⁰ cm⁻³ for silicon — about one
    mobile electron–hole pair per five <em>trillion</em> atoms. That exponential is the
    same tyranny seen in diffusion and Arrhenius chemistry, and it is why intrinsic silicon
    is a terrible conductor (resistivity ~230,000 Ω·cm) and why every real device is doped.
  </p>

  <h2>Doping: the most profitable impurity on Earth</h2>
  <p>
    Substitute phosphorus (5 valence electrons) for a silicon atom (4): four electrons
    complete the bonding; the fifth orbits the extra nuclear charge like a hydrogen atom —
    bound by only ~45 meV (a <strong>shallow donor</strong> level just under the conduction
    band), which room-temperature heat easily pays. Each phosphorus atom donates roughly one
    conduction electron: <strong>n-type</strong> material, where electrons are majority
    carriers. Substitute boron (3 electrons): one bond is left unsatisfied — an acceptor
    level just above the valence band that readily steals an electron from a neighbouring
    bond, creating a mobile hole: <strong>p-type</strong>, holes majority. The arithmetic of
    the effect is the lesson's showpiece. Dope silicon to 10¹⁶ donors per cm³ — about one
    foreign atom in five million — and the electron population rises a <em>millionfold</em>
    over intrinsic; conductivity (σ = q·n·μ, with electron mobility μ ≈ 1350 cm²/V·s)
    gives a resistivity near 0.5 Ω·cm against intrinsic silicon's 230,000 Ω·cm:
    <strong>a factor of ~500,000, from a 0.00002% impurity</strong>. Control is just as
    precise: carrier population obeys the mass-action law n·p = nᵢ², so doping also fixes
    the minority-carrier population, and the Fermi level (the electron chemical potential,
    which sat mid-gap in intrinsic material) shifts toward whichever band the dopant feeds —
    the energy-space thermometer that every device reads.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> doping "adds electrons" the way a bucket fills. The
    crystal stays electrically neutral — every fixed donor ion (+) is balanced by a mobile
    electron (−); what changes is which charges are <em>mobile</em>. That neutrality is
    precisely what makes the junction work: the device is built from immobile, charged
    impurities steering mobile, charged carriers.
  </p>

  <h2>The junction: a boundary that computes</h2>
  <p>
    Join p-type to n-type and mobile carriers diffuse down their gradients — electrons into
    p, holes into n — until each crossing exposes the <strong>fixed</strong> dopant ions
    behind them. That exposed charge strip (the <strong>depletion region</strong>) creates
    an electric field opposing further diffusion; equilibrium arrives at a built-in
    potential of about 0.7 V for silicon, a barrier exactly as tall as the Fermi-level
    difference the doping set. Now the diode exists: <strong>forward bias</strong>
    (positive on p) lowers the barrier and majority carriers flood across — current grows
    as I = I₀·(exp(qV/kT) − 1), which at room temperature means a tenfold current increase
    per ~60 mV: the steepest useful switch curve in technology, and the reason a diode is a
    rectifier, a log-amplifier, a voltage reference and (with a direct band gap) a light
    source — an electron dropping across the gap emits a photon of energy E_g, so gap
    engineering is colour engineering. <strong>Reverse bias</strong> raises the barrier;
    only minority carriers cross, a trickle of nano-to-microamps — until breakdown, when
    the field accelerates carriers enough to multiply them by impact (avalanche) or tunnelling
    (Zener) — deliberately exploited in Zener diodes as voltage references.
  </p>
  <p>
    The transistor is the junction given a third terminal. In a <strong>MOSFET</strong>, a
    metal gate over a thin insulator sits above the channel between source and drain: gate
    voltage electrostatically inverts the channel's surface — pulling majority carriers of
    the opposite type into a thin sheet — and turns conduction on. It is a junction you can
    <em>steer</em>: no gate current (the insulator blocks it), so switching costs almost
    no control energy, which is what lets billions of them sit on one chip. Moore's law was
    the materials sprint to keep that geometry shrinking — high-k dielectrics when SiO₂
    thinned into quantum tunnelling leakage, strained silicon and FinFETs and
    gate-all-around architectures to keep the gate's field in charge of the channel.
  </p>

  <h2>The fab chain: from sand to a billion switches</h2>
  <p>
    The starting material must be the purest solid ever made in quantity: metallurgical
    silicon (98%) is purified to <strong>nine nines</strong> (99.9999999% — one foreign atom
    per billion), because at doping levels of parts per million, uncontrolled parts per
    billion are noise in the recipe. Single crystals grow by the <strong>Czochralski</strong>
    method — a seed crystal drawn slowly from a melt, pulling a dislocation-free ingot —
    sliced into wafers polished to atomic flatness. Devices are built layer by layer:
    <strong>photolithography</strong> prints each layer's pattern (photoresist exposed
    through a mask, developed, then used as a stencil for etching or implantation) — the
    extreme-UV machines that print today's smallest features are the most complex optical
    instruments ever built; <strong>ion implantation</strong> fires dopant ions into the
    surface with mass-spectrometer precision, annealed afterwards to repair the damage (the
    heat-treatment lesson, at nanometre scale); insulating and metal layers follow. Each
    chip is thousands of such cycles deep — a vertical sandwich of patterned films — and the
    yield mathematics (one defect per billion needed, else the chip dies) is why fabs cost
    tens of billions.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Band-gap engineering.</strong> Alloying III–V compounds tunes the gap
    continuously — Al_xGa_(1−x)As sweeps 1.4 to over 2 eV, so emitters and detectors can be
    grown lattice-matched with the gap the application wants; quantum wells confine carriers
    to layers thinner than their wavelength, stepping the effective gap up and sharpening
    laser thresholds. Blue LEDs required decades of "impossible" material work (Nobel 2014:
    Akasaki, Amano, Nakamura — GaN grown on mismatched substrates, p-doping finally achieved
    by electron-beam activation), and with red and green already solved, blue completed
    white solid-state lighting — a Nobel given for a materials-science defect problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Wide-bandgap power electronics.</strong> Bigger gap means the junction survives
    bigger fields before avalanche, so 4H-SiC (3.26 eV) and GaN devices switch at
    10× the voltage and temperature of silicon — EV inverters, fast chargers and grid
    converters are being rebuilt around them, converting higher breakdown field directly
    into system efficiency. The trade is defects: wide-gap growth is harder, and every
    micropipe or dislocation in a power wafer is a premature failure (the failure lesson's
    defect arithmetic, at device scale). Meanwhile 2-D materials (graphene — gapless,
    hence a conductor looking for a switch; MoS₂ and other transition-metal
    dichalcogenides with usable gaps) and oxide semiconductors wait at the research edge of
    the roadmap.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Can doping stay controlled as dimensions shrink toward
    single-atom limits (dopant fluctuation statistics — where exactly are those 100 atoms
    per transistor — becomes a per-device lottery)? Will thermal management, not switching
    physics, become the binding constraint (chips already dissipate hundreds of watts per
    cm² — a heat-spreading problem that is pure materials science)? And can halide
    perovskite solar cells — solution-processed, near-silicon efficiency, defect-tolerant
    band structure — be made to survive twenty years outdoors? The semiconductor lesson's
    fifty-year pattern repeats: every roadblock is a materials problem, and every materials
    fix resets the industry.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Sze &amp; Ng, <em>Physics of Semiconductor Devices</em>; Streetman &amp; Banerjee, <em>Solid State Electronic Devices</em>; the physics ladder's solid-state lesson; Nobel Prize in Physics 2014 (Akasaki, Amano, Nakamura); Czochralski growth literature.</p>
